{{also|cérémonial|ceremoniál}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|cæremonial}} {{qualifier|[[Appendix:Glossary#archaic|archaic]]}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|cerymonial}}, from {{der|enm|la|caerimōniālis}}. 

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en-us-ceremonial.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Of, relating to, or used in a [[ceremony]].
#: {{syn|en|formal|ritual|ritualistic}}
#* {{circa|1593}} {{w|William Shakespeare}}, ''{{w|The Taming of the Shrew}},'' Act III, Scene{{nbsp}}2,<sup>[http://www.opensourceshakespeare.org/views/plays/play_view.php?WorkID=tamingshrew&Scope=entire&pleasewait=1&msg=pl]</sup>
#*: What mockery will it be
#*: To want the bridegroom when the priest attends
#*: To speak the '''ceremonial''' rites of marriage!
#* '''1751''', {{w|Samuel Johnson}}, ''{{w|The Rambler}},'' No. 179, 3{{nbsp}}December, 1751, Volume 6, London: J. Payne and J. Bouquet, 1752, p.{{nbsp}}53,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004772607.0001.006]</sup>
#*: His merit introduced him to splendid tables and elegant acquaintance, but he did not find himself always qualified to join in the conversation. He was distressed by civilities, which he knew not how to repay, and entangled in many '''ceremonial''' perplexities, from which his books and diagrams could not extricate him.
#* '''1827''', {{w|Henry Hallam}}, ''The Constitutional History of England,'' Paris: L. Baudry, Volume 1, Chapter 2, p.{{nbsp}}116,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/constitutionalh22hallgoog]</sup>
#*: {{...}} this change in '''ceremonial''' observances and outward show was trifling when compared to that in the objects of worship {{...}}
#* '''1963''', {{w|Sylvia Plath}}, ''{{w|The Bell Jar}},'' New York: Bantam, 1972, Chapter 15, p.{{nbsp}}151,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/belljar00platrich]</sup>
#*: Philomena Guinea’s black Cadillac eased through the tight, five o’clock traffic like a '''ceremonial''' car.
# {{lb|en|archaic}} Observant of ceremony, ritual, or social [[forms]].
#: {{syn|en|ceremonious}}
#* {{circa|1593}} {{w|John Donne}}, “Satyre I” in ''Poems,'' London: John Marriot, 1633, p.{{nbsp}}326,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A69225.0001.001]</sup>
#*: Oh monstrous, superstitious puritan,
#*: Of refin’d manners, yet '''ceremoniall''' man,
#* '''1693''', {{w|John Dryden}} (translator), ''{{w|Satires (Juvenal)|The Satires}} of {{w|Juvenal|Decimus Junius Juvenalis}},'' London: Jacob Tonson, “The Tenth Satyr,” lines 56-57, p.{{nbsp}}193,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A46439.0001.001]</sup>
#*: {{...}} with dumb Pride, and a set formal Face,
#*: He moves, in the dull '''Ceremonial''' track,

